{"author":"rebar review bot","author_email":"rebar-review-bot@rebar.solutions.navateam.com","data":{"body":"## AC4 withdrawn from the shipped surface — change 3014 abandoned\n\nOperator decision: abandon, do not rework.\n\nGerrit change 3014 is abandoned, for the same reason change 2608 was. Both\nimplemented AC4 as it was written, and AC4 as written required reading\n`~/.local/state/rebar-dev/` — the reject-streak counter and `current` pointer\nproduced by this host's main-tracking LaunchAgent. That setup is documented in\n`docs/local-env-main-tracking.local.md`, git-ignored because it is one\noperator's machine configuration rather than rebar behaviour. A `rebar doctor`\nscan for it, plus a `docs/user-guide.md` section, would ship that configuration\nas product.\n\nThe two rejections came from opposite directions, which is worth recording\nbecause it shows the fault was in the AC rather than in either implementation:\n\n- **2608** — operator `Code-Review-1`, abandoned. The detector read the\n  machine-local state files directly.\n- **3014 patch set 1** — `LLM-Review-1`. `scan_build_freshness(repo_root=...)`\n  measured the updater's published SHA against the *doctor caller's* store repo,\n  comparing two unrelated repositories.\n- **3014 patch set 2** — `LLM-Review-1` again, scope-intent. The fix for patch\n  set 1 added an updater-state `source-repo` lookup, which moved further into\n  the machine-local dependency rather than out of it.\n\nThe rescope promised in 2608's abandon message (\"I am rescoping AC4\") was never\nwritten into this ticket's description. That is why the second attempt\nreproduced the first one's rejection: the text being implemented still demanded\nthe rejected design. AC4 has now been rewritten to record the withdrawal, and is\ntagged `[non-codebase]` because its done-evidence is a decision on those two\nGerrit changes, not an artifact in this tree.\n\n## The general capability is a separate ticket, deliberately not tracked here\n\nThe 2608 decision distinguished two things that had been conflated: a SPECIFIC\ndetector for this host's updater, which does not belong in the shipped surface\nat all, and a GENERAL check — warn when the installed rebar is far behind `main`\n— which could affect any developer with a stale install.\n\nThe general one remains plausible and needs its own ticket. It would not start\nfrom scratch: `rebar.llm.build_drift.running_build_sha()` already resolves the\nrunning build's commit, and `BuildDrift` already carries `commits_behind`\nagainst a pinned ref with full 40-character SHAs. This very session saw it fire\nunprompted while closing `794e`:\n\n```\nWARNING rebar.llm.build_drift: this rebar build (e900fa14a, 2026-09-19, dirty)\nis 25 commit(s) BEHIND the ref this gate pinned (204c60697)\n```\n\nIts only caller today is `gate_source.py`, so it runs only inside a gate that\npins a ref. Surfacing it elsewhere is the feature; nothing new needs inventing,\nand no machine-local path is involved.\n\n## What remains on this ticket\n\nAC3 is already WITHDRAWN by operator decision, and AC4 is now withdrawn too.\nThat leaves AC1/AC2 (the updater's kickstart and uv selection, which the 2608\nabandon message records as already resolved — the updater had failed 122\nconsecutive runs and is publishing current builds again) and **AC5**, the\npruner's `Directory not empty` failures and the partial-directory cleanup. AC5\nis untouched by any of this and is the live remaining work."},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1789938330420488001,"uuid":"7917b1d9-45f6-49ed-aa76-f0f51da89822"}